    s__HOT_FAULT_LEAK_ANSWER(py_str("Turn the cold feed off at the valve on the pipe into the tank and switch the immersion off at its wall spur, then open the hot taps to take the water level down; the tank holds scalding water, so let it cool before you touch it. A drip at a nut or a fitting is often a case of nipping the compression nut up a quarter turn, but if the body of the cylinder is wet, or the foam lagging is soaked through, the tank has gone and a plumber swaps it - that is a day's work, and it is not a job to leave running. Catch the water in a bucket or a towel, and if it is coming through a ceiling, turn the water off at the stopcock as well."));
    s__HOT_FAULT_COLD_ANSWER(py_str("Check the immersion heater's own fused spur switch on the wall first - the little switch beside the tank with a fuse in it - and then the timer or programmer, because a tank on a timer that has been knocked is the commonest reason for cold water. If the spur is on, take the cap off the immersion and press the red cut-out button on top of it, which trips when the element overheats. Still cold, or the fuse blows again, means the element has gone: an electrician or plumber drains the tank and swaps it in about an hour, and it is usually pounds rather than hundreds."));
