    s__HOT_FAULT_TEST_ANSWER(py_str("Test the immersion in three steps, with the power off at the spur each time you touch the cap. First the supply: is the fused spur switched on and is its fuse sound, or does the breaker stay up when you reset it. Then the thermostat: take the round cap off the top of the cylinder, turn the dial right up and listen - a click means the thermostat works, and a cut-out button that has popped out means it tripped on overheat and needs pressing back in. Then the element itself: with the spur off and the two element wires off, a multimeter across the terminals should read somewhere between about 15 and 30 ohms; no reading at all means the element has gone."));
    s__HOT_FAULT_TRIP_ANSWER(py_str("Stop resetting it - an immersion element that trips the breaker has gone electrically, and resetting it each time is how a fuse board gets hot. Leave the circuit off at the spur, and get it swapped: the tank is drained, the old element unscrewed and a new one fitted, an hour's work for a plumber or electrician. Until then the boiler or the back-up heating is what you have for hot water."));
